{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xc8gpPuZJ8wL1orfcFbMYh","type":"tool_result","content":"# Changelog\n\nWhat changed in Pixel Engine for a game built on it, newest first. The version is\n`package.json`'s; a game reads it as `ENGINE_VERSION`, `Engine.version` or\n`engine.state().version`. The engine kit (`npm run bundle`, docs/GUIDE.md) ships this file.\n\nWhen a change alters the public API (`src/bundle.ts` exports) or something a game can see,\nbump the version (minor while it is 0.x) and add its section here; `npm test` fails when\n`package.json`'s version has no section.\n\n## 0.46.0\n\n- **No more handoff page**: the engine's site is gone, so `manifest.json` has no `handoff` and the\n  kit's `README.md` no longer names a page for agents. Newer kits are where they were:\n  `latest` (the newest release's zip), `releases` and `source`.\n\n## 0.45.0\n\n- **The kit runs in Node**: `Engine.headless(game, { assets })` from `pixel-engine.js` alone, with\n  no `node_modules`. In Node, Rapier's wasm comes from the data URL the kit inlines, and a reader\n  (`assets`, `setAssetReader`) no longer sees the files the kit embeds (`__PIXEL_ASSETS__`: the\n  built-in models load as they do in a page).\n- **`node check.mjs --headless game.js`**: plays a game module in Node, no browser. It makes the game\n  with the module's `createGame()` export, plays the `--keys` frames, replays the recording in a\n  fresh game and writes `report.json` (`state()`, errors, the fingerprint). Exit 1 on an error or a\n  replay that ends elsewhere, 2 for a page or a module without `createGame()`.\n- **The starter `game.js` exports `createGame()`** and starts the engine only where there is a\n  `document`, so it imports in Node. GUIDE.md section 8, *Play it in Node*, shows a test of your own.\n\n## 0.44.0\n\n- **Removed from the texture library: its equipment icons**: `sword-icon`, `axe-icon`,\n  `mace-icon`, `dagger-icon`, `bow-icon`, `staff-icon`, `wand-icon`, `shield-icon`,\n  `round-shield-icon`, `quiver-icon`, `helmet-icon`, `armor-icon`, `gloves-icon`, `boots-icon`,\n  `belt-icon`, `ring` and `amulet`. They were a second way to draw an item next to Riftlight's own\n  icons, which fill any item footprint (a one-cell item's box is 7 px wide), and nothing used them. A game that wants one makes its own\n  `TextureDef` (docs/GUIDE-ASSETS.md) and adds it with `TEXTURE_LIBRARY.add(def)`. The library\n  keeps the items Riftlight has no class for (potions, key, scroll, map, book, bomb, torch...),\n  `gem-icon`, `gold-icon`, the skill and UI icons and the creatures.\n\n## 0.43.0\n\n- **`Game.args`**: the plain JSON a game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a\n  lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by). Recordings keep it (**`Recording.args`**),\n  so a tool can make the same game again and replay one (`npm run replay` in this repository).\n\n## 0.42.0\n\n- **`Engine.headless(game, options)`: games without a page** (docs/engine/headless.md). The same\n  boot, game code and frames as `Engine.start`, in Node: no GPU, DOM or listeners, manual time from\n  the start (`step()`, `input.setKey` / `send`, `replay()`), errors in `engine.errors`. Options add\n  `viewport` (default 960×540 at DPR 1) and `assets`.\n- **`engine.renderer` is a `View`**: what gameplay reads from a view (art resolution, framing,\n  `viewport`, look, mode, filters, `screen`, `capture()`), the page's `PixelRenderer` or a\n  `HeadlessView`. Direct GPU work goes through **`engine.gpu`** (the `PixelRenderer`, null\n  headless): `engine.renderer.renderer` becomes `engine.gpu.renderer`. `BackendName` gains\n  `'headless'`.\n- **The GPU helpers take the view**: `swarm.update(engine.renderer, dt)` (was\n  `engine.renderer.renderer`), `mirror.attach(engine.renderer, scene)` and\n  `view.attach(engine.renderer, scene)` as before; headless they do nothing.\n- New exports: `HeadlessOptions`, `View`, `HeadlessView` (`setViewport`), `HeadlessViewOptions`,\n  `HEADLESS_VIEWPORT`, `frameView`, `Viewport`, `setAssetReader`, `AssetReader`.\n- **Assets without `fetch`**: `setAssetReader(read)` reads models and packs through\n  `(path) => Promise<ArrayBuffer>` for the whole process (`AssetReader`); `nodeAssets()` in\n  `src/engine/node.ts` reads `public/` in Node. A model load that fails is tried again on the\n  next call instead of failing from the cache.\n- **`Recording.view`**: the viewport a recording was made on. A headless replay frames for it; a\n  page warns when its own differs (pointer aim and the HUD follow it). `frameView` and `Viewport`\n  are the framing both views share.\n- The HUD's `width` / `height` follow the view from the first frame, before anything is drawn.\n\n## 0.41.0\n\n- **One readiness signal: `window.__PIXEL_READY__`.** A promise that exists as soon as the engine\n  loads, resolves with the engine when `Engine.start` has finished and rejects with the reason a\n  start failed. Tools await it instead of polling for `__PIXEL_ENGINE__`.\n- **`Game.ready(ctx)`.** An optional hook the engine calls when it starts running a game (just\n  before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`: put a game's page handles for tools\n  there, so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception in it is reported like any\n  hook's (`engine.errors`, on screen) and the game runs on. A game whose class already had a\n  `ready` member that isn't this hook must rename it.\n- **`engine.step()` before the first start throws** (`engine not ready: await\n  window.__PIXEL_READY__`) instead of running 0 frames and leaving the game frozen in manual time.\n  During a later `loadGame` it still runs 0 frames.\n\n## 0.40.0\n\n- **No engine hotkeys in a shipped game.** P, R, \\`, [ ], M are developer tools: on with the debug\n  panel (the dev server, `?debug=1`), off otherwise. `debugKeys: true` / `false` turns them on /\n  off always; an object (`DebugKeyChanges`: `{ resolution: 'F2' }`) still moves or drops single\n  keys, and no longer turns them on. `resolveDebugKeys()` with nothing now gives none (`true`: the\n  defaults), and `rebindDebugKeys(keys, changes)` moves only the keys that are on, so a game that\n  moves R off its own key at runtime never turns the hotkeys back on. The debug panel lists only\n  the hotkeys that are on, under their keys, and the touch bar's debug button is ⓘ (⚙ is the\n  settings).\n- **Player settings** (`engine.settings`, `PlayerSettings`): look, pixel art or smooth 3D, pixel\n  size, quality, frame rate and debug info, as plain data saved per game (`EngineOptions.settings.key`,\n  default the first game's name) and applied over the game's own choices. A field left out is the\n  game's; `set`, `reset`, `replace`, `values`, `onChange`. A review flag in the URL wins over a\n  saved value at start (which stays saved), and a chosen look wins over each new level's own. A\n  replay saves nothing and puts the player's settings back when it ends.\n- **The settings panel**, with the sound volumes, opens from the ⚙ button (`settings.button`: a\n  corner, default top-right; on a mouse it shows while the pointer moves; `false` for none) or\n  `engine.settings.open()`. It pauses the game while open, keys pressed in it never reach the\n  game, and it stays shut while a replay plays. Its Look is off while pixel art is: smooth 3D\n  draws no look. Also `engine.useQuality(option)` (`auto`: the device's level, lowered once by a\n  measurement), `engine.showDebug(on)`, `engine.debugShown` and `audio.resetVolumes()`.\n\n## 0.39.0\n\nThe kit as GitHub Releases. No API change.\n\n- **The kit as a GitHub Release, from this version on:** the version on main's newest commit is\n  published once its CI passes (a version superseded on main before then is skipped), as\n  `engine-v<version>` with `pixel-engine.zip` (it unzips to `pixel-engine/`) and that version's\n  section of this changelog as its notes. The newest is always\n  https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip; it\n  downloads from github.com and release-assets.githubusercontent.com, both on Claude Code cloud's\n  default network allowlist.\n- **`manifest.json`'s `latest` is now the newest release's zip**, and `releases` lists every\n  release (GUIDE.md, section 10). The site's kit (`/engine.zip`) is still rebuilt on every merge.\n\n## 0.38.0\n\nThe kit, for an agent handed the engine (the engine repository's README.md, *Handing the engine to\nanother agent*). No API change.\n\n- **Where newer versions are.** `manifest.json` has `latest` (the newest kit's URL), `handoff` (the\n  page for agents) and `source` (the repository that builds it), and the kit's `README.md` says\n  how to build the kit from a clone when that URL can't be reached (GUIDE.md, section 10).\n- **`check.mjs` finds a global Playwright** (`npm i -g playwright`; Claude Code cloud sessions come\n  with one) when none is installed beside the game.\n- GUIDE.md: the engine's hotkeys fire on R, P and M as well as the game's own action (move or turn\n  them off with `debugKeys`), and `step()` draws nothing, so a page screenshot still shows the old\n  frame.\n\n## 0.37.0\n\n- **WebGPU only.** The engine renders with WebGPU and nothing else: the WebGL 2 fallback is gone.\n  A browser without WebGPU (no `navigator.gpu`, no adapter, or three falling back to WebGL 2) is","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b716b92-81c2-4c62-8381-9131b2f861f1","uuid":"15b1da3c-b8f9-4a25-a59f-6cf2f954c293","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:20:18.720Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Changelog\n\nWhat changed in Pixel Engine for a game built on it, newest first. The version is\n`package.json`'s; a game reads it as `ENGINE_VERSION`, `Engine.version` or\n`engine.state().version`. The engine kit (`npm run bundle`, docs/GUIDE.md) ships this file.\n\nWhen a change alters the public API (`src/bundle.ts` exports) or something a game can see,\nbump the version (minor while it is 0.x) and add its section here; `npm test` fails when\n`package.json`'s version has no section.\n\n## 0.46.0\n\n- **No more handoff page**: the engine's site is gone, so `manifest.json` has no `handoff` and the\n  kit's `README.md` no longer names a page for agents. Newer kits are where they were:\n  `latest` (the newest release's zip), `releases` and `source`.\n\n## 0.45.0\n\n- **The kit runs in Node**: `Engine.headless(game, { assets })` from `pixel-engine.js` alone, with\n  no `node_modules`. In Node, Rapier's wasm comes from the data URL the kit inlines, and a reader\n  (`assets`, `setAssetReader`) no longer sees the files the kit embeds (`__PIXEL_ASSETS__`: the\n  built-in models load as they do in a page).\n- **`node check.mjs --headless game.js`**: plays a game module in Node, no browser. It makes the game\n  with the module's `createGame()` export, plays the `--keys` frames, replays the recording in a\n  fresh game and writes `report.json` (`state()`, errors, the fingerprint). Exit 1 on an error or a\n  replay that ends elsewhere, 2 for a page or a module without `createGame()`.\n- **The starter `game.js` exports `createGame()`** and starts the engine only where there is a\n  `document`, so it imports in Node. GUIDE.md section 8, *Play it in Node*, shows a test of your own.\n\n## 0.44.0\n\n- **Removed from the texture library: its equipment icons**: `sword-icon`, `axe-icon`,\n  `mace-icon`, `dagger-icon`, `bow-icon`, `staff-icon`, `wand-icon`, `shield-icon`,\n  `round-shield-icon`, `quiver-icon`, `helmet-icon`, `armor-icon`, `gloves-icon`, `boots-icon`,\n  `belt-icon`, `ring` and `amulet`. They were a second way to draw an item next to Riftlight's own\n  icons, which fill any item footprint (a one-cell item's box is 7 px wide), and nothing used them. A game that wants one makes its own\n  `TextureDef` (docs/GUIDE-ASSETS.md) and adds it with `TEXTURE_LIBRARY.add(def)`. The library\n  keeps the items Riftlight has no class for (potions, key, scroll, map, book, bomb, torch...),\n  `gem-icon`, `gold-icon`, the skill and UI icons and the creatures.\n\n## 0.43.0\n\n- **`Game.args`**: the plain JSON a game was made with, when its name alone can't rebuild it (a\n  lab's depth and seed, the door a room was entered by). Recordings keep it (**`Recording.args`**),\n  so a tool can make the same game again and replay one (`npm run replay` in this repository).\n\n## 0.42.0\n\n- **`Engine.headless(game, options)`: games without a page** (docs/engine/headless.md). The same\n  boot, game code and frames as `Engine.start`, in Node: no GPU, DOM or listeners, manual time from\n  the start (`step()`, `input.setKey` / `send`, `replay()`), errors in `engine.errors`. Options add\n  `viewport` (default 960×540 at DPR 1) and `assets`.\n- **`engine.renderer` is a `View`**: what gameplay reads from a view (art resolution, framing,\n  `viewport`, look, mode, filters, `screen`, `capture()`), the page's `PixelRenderer` or a\n  `HeadlessView`. Direct GPU work goes through **`engine.gpu`** (the `PixelRenderer`, null\n  headless): `engine.renderer.renderer` becomes `engine.gpu.renderer`. `BackendName` gains\n  `'headless'`.\n- **The GPU helpers take the view**: `swarm.update(engine.renderer, dt)` (was\n  `engine.renderer.renderer`), `mirror.attach(engine.renderer, scene)` and\n  `view.attach(engine.renderer, scene)` as before; headless they do nothing.\n- New exports: `HeadlessOptions`, `View`, `HeadlessView` (`setViewport`), `HeadlessViewOptions`,\n  `HEADLESS_VIEWPORT`, `frameView`, `Viewport`, `setAssetReader`, `AssetReader`.\n- **Assets without `fetch`**: `setAssetReader(read)` reads models and packs through\n  `(path) => Promise<ArrayBuffer>` for the whole process (`AssetReader`); `nodeAssets()` in\n  `src/engine/node.ts` reads `public/` in Node. A model load that fails is tried again on the\n  next call instead of failing from the cache.\n- **`Recording.view`**: the viewport a recording was made on. A headless replay frames for it; a\n  page warns when its own differs (pointer aim and the HUD follow it). `frameView` and `Viewport`\n  are the framing both views share.\n- The HUD's `width` / `height` follow the view from the first frame, before anything is drawn.\n\n## 0.41.0\n\n- **One readiness signal: `window.__PIXEL_READY__`.** A promise that exists as soon as the engine\n  loads, resolves with the engine when `Engine.start` has finished and rejects with the reason a\n  start failed. Tools await it instead of polling for `__PIXEL_ENGINE__`.\n- **`Game.ready(ctx)`.** An optional hook the engine calls when it starts running a game (just\n  before `__PIXEL_READY__` resolves) and after each `loadGame`: put a game's page handles for tools\n  there, so they never exist before the engine can step. An exception in it is reported like any\n  hook's (`engine.errors`, on screen) and the game runs on. A game whose class already had a\n  `ready` member that isn't this hook must rename it.\n- **`engine.step()` before the first start throws** (`engine not ready: await\n  window.__PIXEL_READY__`) instead of running 0 frames and leaving the game frozen in manual time.\n  During a later `loadGame` it still runs 0 frames.\n\n## 0.40.0\n\n- **No engine hotkeys in a shipped game.** P, R, \\`, [ ], M are developer tools: on with the debug\n  panel (the dev server, `?debug=1`), off otherwise. `debugKeys: true` / `false` turns them on /\n  off always; an object (`DebugKeyChanges`: `{ resolution: 'F2' }`) still moves or drops single\n  keys, and no longer turns them on. `resolveDebugKeys()` with nothing now gives none (`true`: the\n  defaults), and `rebindDebugKeys(keys, changes)` moves only the keys that are on, so a game that\n  moves R off its own key at runtime never turns the hotkeys back on. The debug panel lists only\n  the hotkeys that are on, under their keys, and the touch bar's debug button is ⓘ (⚙ is the\n  settings).\n- **Player settings** (`engine.settings`, `PlayerSettings`): look, pixel art or smooth 3D, pixel\n  size, quality, frame rate and debug info, as plain data saved per game (`EngineOptions.settings.key`,\n  default the first game's name) and applied over the game's own choices. A field left out is the\n  game's; `set`, `reset`, `replace`, `values`, `onChange`. A review flag in the URL wins over a\n  saved value at start (which stays saved), and a chosen look wins over each new level's own. A\n  replay saves nothing and puts the player's settings back when it ends.\n- **The settings panel**, with the sound volumes, opens from the ⚙ button (`settings.button`: a\n  corner, default top-right; on a mouse it shows while the pointer moves; `false` for none) or\n  `engine.settings.open()`. It pauses the game while open, keys pressed in it never reach the\n  game, and it stays shut while a replay plays. Its Look is off while pixel art is: smooth 3D\n  draws no look. Also `engine.useQuality(option)` (`auto`: the device's level, lowered once by a\n  measurement), `engine.showDebug(on)`, `engine.debugShown` and `audio.resetVolumes()`.\n\n## 0.39.0\n\nThe kit as GitHub Releases. No API change.\n\n- **The kit as a GitHub Release, from this version on:** the version on main's newest commit is\n  published once its CI passes (a version superseded on main before then is skipped), as\n  `engine-v<version>` with `pixel-engine.zip` (it unzips to `pixel-engine/`) and that version's\n  section of this changelog as its notes. The newest is always\n  https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip; it\n  downloads from github.com and release-assets.githubusercontent.com, both on Claude Code cloud's\n  default network allowlist.\n- **`manifest.json`'s `latest` is now the newest release's zip**, and `releases` lists every\n  release (GUIDE.md, section 10). The site's kit (`/engine.zip`) is still rebuilt on every merge.\n\n## 0.38.0\n\nThe kit, for an agent handed the engine (the engine repository's README.md, *Handing the engine to\nanother agent*). No API change.\n\n- **Where newer versions are.** `manifest.json` has `latest` (the newest kit's URL), `handoff` (the\n  page for agents) and `source` (the repository that builds it), and the kit's `README.md` says\n  how to build the kit from a clone when that URL can't be reached (GUIDE.md, section 10).\n- **`check.mjs` finds a global Playwright** (`npm i -g playwright`; Claude Code cloud sessions come\n  with one) when none is installed beside the game.\n- GUIDE.md: the engine's hotkeys fire on R, P and M as well as the game's own action (move or turn\n  them off with `debugKeys`), and `step()` draws nothing, so a page screenshot still shows the old\n  frame.\n\n## 0.37.0\n\n- **WebGPU only.** The engine renders with WebGPU and nothing else: the WebGL 2 fallback is gone.\n  A browser without WebGPU (no `navigator.gpu`, no adapter, or three falling back to WebGL 2) is","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Xc8gpPuZJ8wL1orfcFbMYh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
