evice coordinates (−1..1, y up, as `input.pointer` gives them): where it starts (on the near plane) and its unit direction. For picking things with the mouse, or aiming at it. An orthographic rig stands far b…\n625:Eyes of the character: mouse look (click to lock the pointer) or Q/E to turn. No zoom.\n1421:- `readonly tiles: Crumble[]`\n1428:- `crumble(o: CrumbleOptions): Crumble`: A floor tile that drops shortly after you step on it, and grows back.\n1543:### interface Crumble\n1545:- `readonly o: CrumbleOptions`\n1551:### interface CrumbleOptions\n2253:A pointer button pressed or let go (`Input.buttonEvents`): its key, and where the pointer was (normalised device coordinates).\n2268:### GAMEPAD_BUTTONS\n2271:const GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>\n2274:Standard-mapping gamepad buttons → the key codes they press (see character/controls.ts\n2275:KEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \"standard\" layout:\n2278:### interface GamepadLike\n2280:The parts of the Gamepad API that Input reads (tests can pass plain objects).\n2289:Keyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n2292:- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n2293:  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n2294:  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n2295:- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n2296:  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n2303:- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n2304:- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's