{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PvptMiUyoyLtMrHyoXLGsF","type":"tool_result","content":"### CAMERA_PRESETS\n\n```ts\nconst CAMERA_PRESETS: readonly CameraPreset[]\n```\n\n### interface CameraConfig\n\n- `preset?: CameraPreset`\n- `zoom?: number`: Zoom factor, 1 = preset default, >1 = closer. Ignored by `first`.\n- `minZoom?: number`\n- `maxZoom?: number`\n- `viewHeight?: number`: Ortho presets: visible world height at zoom 1.\n- `pitch?: number`: iso: degrees below horizon / around Y.\n- `yaw?: number`\n- `fov?: number`: Perspective presets: vertical field of view in degrees.\n- `distance?: number`: third: orbit distance at zoom 1.\n- `position?: [number, number, number]`: fixed: camera position and look-at target.\n- `target?: [number, number, number]`\n- `projection?: 'perspective' | 'ortho'`: fixed/free: projection.\n- `stiffness?: number`: Follow smoothing; higher is snappier.\n\n### type CameraPreset\n\n```ts\ntype CameraPreset = 'iso' | 'topdown' | 'side' | 'third' | 'first' | 'free' | 'fixed'\n```\n\nCamera presets. A game picks ONE at startup (EngineOptions.camera / `?camera=`). For\nreviewing, `engine.setCamera()` swaps presets live without touching the world. `free`\nis an authoring tool: fly around, press Enter to fix the view, and paste the printed\n`fixed` config into your game.\n\n### class CameraRig\n\nBase rig: owns the three.js camera, zoom and the ground-plane movement basis.\n\n- `new CameraRig(config: CameraConfig)`\n- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n- `readonly camera: OrthographicCamera | PerspectiveCamera`\n- `readonly focus: Vector3`: World point the rig is centred on (used for shadow framing).\n- `zoom: number`\n- `readonly minZoom: number`\n- `readonly maxZoom: number`\n- `readonly zoomable: boolean`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n- `readonly hidesTarget: boolean`: First person hides the player model.\n- `readonly lockDepth: boolean`: Side-scroller: characters should stay on their depth lane.\n- `controlsCharacter: boolean`: False while the free camera is flying (input drives the camera, not the player).\n- `snap: boolean`: Pixel-snap the camera (ortho only).\n- `aspect: number`: Viewport aspect (width / height), following the art resolution (see `setAspect`).\n- `rayAt(x: number, y: number, origin: Vector3, dir: Vector3): void`: The ray through screen point (x, y) in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up, as `input.pointer` gives them): where it starts (on the near plane) and its unit direction. For picking things with the mouse, or aiming at it. An orthographic rig stands far b…\n- `setZoom(zoom: number): void`\n- `setAspect(aspect: number): void`: Match the art resolution's aspect (adaptive framing changes it with the screen). `update()` calls this from `CameraUpdate.resolution`; the engine also calls it on swap.\n- `teleport(target: Vector3): void`: Place the camera without smoothing.\n- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3 }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n- `update(u: CameraUpdate): void`\n- `describe(): Record<string, unknown>`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n- `state(): CameraRigState`: The rig as data, exact (`engine.snapshot()`).\n- `setState(s: CameraRigState): void`: Put the rig back where `state()` found it (the same preset).\n\n### interface CameraRigState\n\nWhere a rig is and looks, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): the frame after a restore sees the camera the frame after the snapshot did.\n\n- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n- `readonly focus: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly zoom: number`\n- `readonly aspect: number`\n- `readonly position: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly quaternion: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly shaken: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly own: Readonly<Record<string, number>>`: What a preset keeps of its own (an orbit's yaw, pitch and distance).\n\n### createCameraRig()\n\n```ts\nfunction createCameraRig(config: CameraConfig = {}): CameraRig\n\n460:### class Settings\n461-\n462-`engine.settings`: the player's settings, applied to the engine and saved. A field the page URL\n463-sets (`?look=`, `?mode=`, `?res=`, `?quality=`, `?fps=`, `?debug=`: review flags) wins over a\n464-saved one at start (which stays saved). `open`, `close` and `toggle` show and hide the\n465-settings panel.\n466-\n467-- `new Settings(host: SettingsHost, key: string | null)`\n468-- `readonly key: string | null`\n469-- `get values: Readonly<PlayerSettings>`: The player's choices in effect: fields left out are the game's (or a review flag's).\n470-- `get isOpen: boolean`\n471-- `set(patch: PlayerSettings): void`: Change some settings: applied at once and saved. A field set to `undefined` goes back to the game's.\n472-- `reset(): void`: Forget every choice, the held ones too: the game's own picture again.\n473-- `replace(values: PlayerSettings): void`: Make `values` the settings, changing only the fields that differ: the ones it leaves out go back to the game's.\n474-- `onChange(fn: (values: Readonly<PlayerSettings>) => void): () => void`: Called with the values after every change; returns the unsubscribe.\n475-- `open(): void`\n476-- `close(): void`\n477-- `toggle(): void`\n478-\n479-### interface SettingsHost\n480-\n481-What the settings change in the engine (Engine implements it; tests pass a fake).\n482-\n--\n492:### class SettingsPanel\n493-\n494-The settings panel (DOM, like the debug panel: it stays legible at any art resolution) and the\n495-⚙ button that opens it: the player's picture, sound and engine options (`engine.settings`,\n496-settings.ts; the AudioManager's volumes). The engine pauses the game while it is open. Keys\n497-pressed in it never reach the game; Escape or Done closes it.\n498-\n499-- `new SettingsPanel(engine: Engine, container: HTMLElement, corner: TouchCorner | false)`\n500-- `readonly root: HTMLDivElement`\n501-- `readonly button: HTMLButtonElement | null`\n502-- `show(): void`: Show the panel with the engine's current values. The panel takes the focus, not a control: a game key pressed out of habit (D, Space) must not pick an option; Tab reaches the controls.\n503-- `hide(): void`\n504-- `sync(): void`: Put the engine's current values into the controls, and into the debug panel (the loop fills it only while the game runs).\n505-- `dispose(): void`\n506-\n507-### storeSettings()\n508-\n509-```ts\n510-function storeSettings(key: string, settings: PlayerSettings): void\n511-```\n512-\n513-### urlSettings()\n514-\n358:### interface PlayerSettings\n359-\n360-The player's settings: the standard picture and engine options every game gets, as plain data\n361-(sound is the AudioManager's, which keeps its own volumes). A field left out is the game's own\n362-choice; a field set is the player's and wins over it. `engine.settings` holds them, applies\n363-them, saves them per game and shows them in the settings panel (the ⚙ button).\n364-\n365-- `look?: string`: A LOOK_PRESETS name. Left out: the look the game gives each level.\n366-- `pixelArt?: boolean`: Pixel art (the pixel pass), or false for smooth 3D (render mode `raw`).\n367-- `pixelSize?: 'fine' | 'chunky'`: The art resolution: `fine` (the game's, 480×270 by default) or `chunky` (two thirds of it: bigger pixels).\n368-- `quality?: QualityOption`: Shadow and light quality: a level, or `auto` (the device's).\n369-- `fps?: number`: Frame cap: 60, 30, or 0 for the display's own rate.\n370-- `debugInfo?: boolean`: The debug panel: frame rate, camera, GPU errors and the game's status.\n371-\n372-### QUALITY\n1368:### toonMaterial()\n1369-\n1370-```ts\n1371-function toonMaterial(color: ColorRepresentation, options: ToonMaterialOptions = {}): MeshToonNodeMaterial\n1372-```\n1373-\n1374-Shared 3-band toon node material for a palette color, optionally textured and/or\n1375-vertex-colored (color × map × vertex color), cached per combination. Untextured ones\n1376-(and ones whose texture is itself shared, e.g. from a cached GLB) are marked\n1377-`userData.shared`, so level unloads keep them; a material with a level's own texture is\n1378-disposed with the level, and disposing it drops it from the cache.\n1379-\n1380-### interface ToonMaterialOptions\n1381-\n1382-- `map?: Texture | null`: Base color texture. Switched to nearest filtering (crisp texels, no mip blur).\n1383-- `vertexColors?: boolean`: Multiply by the geometry's `color` attribute (vertex colors).\n1384-- `rim?: number`: A hard-banded rim light in this colour (hex) on grazing, upward-facing surfaces: a silhouette that reads against dark floors (monsters in dark levels). Added to the emissive, so `material.emissive` (hit flashes) still works on top. Same shader for every rim\n1385-- `dissolve?: boolean`: Pixel dissolve: the material reads its own `dissolve` property (0 = solid, 1 = gone) and discards a per-art-pixel noise pattern below it, with a 1-pixel glowing front in the rim colour (or white). Set it on per-body clones (`material.dissolve = 0.4`),\n1386-\n1387-## 5. Physics\n1388-\n1389-### interface BoxOptions\n1390-\n1380:### interface ToonMaterialOptions\n1381-\n1382-- `map?: Texture | null`: Base color texture. Switched to nearest filtering (crisp texels, no mip blur).\n1383-- `vertexColors?: boolean`: Multiply by the geometry's `color` attribute (vertex colors).\n1384-- `rim?: number`: A hard-banded rim light in this colour (hex) on grazing, upward-facing surfaces: a silhouette that reads against dark floors (monsters in dark levels). Added to the emissive, so `material.emissive` (hit flashes) still works on top. Same shader for every rim\n1385-- `dissolve?: boolean`: Pixel dissolve: the material reads its own `dissolve` property (0 = solid, 1 = gone) and discards a per-art-pixel noise pattern below it, with a 1-pixel glowing front in the rim colour (or white). Set it on per-body clones (`material.dissolve = 0.4`),\n1386-\n1387-## 5. Physics\n1388-","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b716b92-81c2-4c62-8381-9131b2f861f1","uuid":"56ac9f6a-4fc0-480e-a676-dc9e99b13e38","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:26:43.912Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"### CAMERA_PRESETS\n\n```ts\nconst CAMERA_PRESETS: readonly CameraPreset[]\n```\n\n### interface CameraConfig\n\n- `preset?: CameraPreset`\n- `zoom?: number`: Zoom factor, 1 = preset default, >1 = closer. Ignored by `first`.\n- `minZoom?: number`\n- `maxZoom?: number`\n- `viewHeight?: number`: Ortho presets: visible world height at zoom 1.\n- `pitch?: number`: iso: degrees below horizon / around Y.\n- `yaw?: number`\n- `fov?: number`: Perspective presets: vertical field of view in degrees.\n- `distance?: number`: third: orbit distance at zoom 1.\n- `position?: [number, number, number]`: fixed: camera position and look-at target.\n- `target?: [number, number, number]`\n- `projection?: 'perspective' | 'ortho'`: fixed/free: projection.\n- `stiffness?: number`: Follow smoothing; higher is snappier.\n\n### type CameraPreset\n\n```ts\ntype CameraPreset = 'iso' | 'topdown' | 'side' | 'third' | 'first' | 'free' | 'fixed'\n```\n\nCamera presets. A game picks ONE at startup (EngineOptions.camera / `?camera=`). For\nreviewing, `engine.setCamera()` swaps presets live without touching the world. `free`\nis an authoring tool: fly around, press Enter to fix the view, and paste the printed\n`fixed` config into your game.\n\n### class CameraRig\n\nBase rig: owns the three.js camera, zoom and the ground-plane movement basis.\n\n- `new CameraRig(config: CameraConfig)`\n- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n- `readonly camera: OrthographicCamera | PerspectiveCamera`\n- `readonly focus: Vector3`: World point the rig is centred on (used for shadow framing).\n- `zoom: number`\n- `readonly minZoom: number`\n- `readonly maxZoom: number`\n- `readonly zoomable: boolean`: Whether zoom applies (all presets except first person).\n- `readonly hidesTarget: boolean`: First person hides the player model.\n- `readonly lockDepth: boolean`: Side-scroller: characters should stay on their depth lane.\n- `controlsCharacter: boolean`: False while the free camera is flying (input drives the camera, not the player).\n- `snap: boolean`: Pixel-snap the camera (ortho only).\n- `aspect: number`: Viewport aspect (width / height), following the art resolution (see `setAspect`).\n- `rayAt(x: number, y: number, origin: Vector3, dir: Vector3): void`: The ray through screen point (x, y) in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up, as `input.pointer` gives them): where it starts (on the near plane) and its unit direction. For picking things with the mouse, or aiming at it. An orthographic rig stands far b…\n- `setZoom(zoom: number): void`\n- `setAspect(aspect: number): void`: Match the art resolution's aspect (adaptive framing changes it with the screen). `update()` calls this from `CameraUpdate.resolution`; the engine also calls it on swap.\n- `teleport(target: Vector3): void`: Place the camera without smoothing.\n- `groundBasis(): { right: Vector3; forward: Vector3 }`: Screen-relative movement basis on the ground plane.\n- `update(u: CameraUpdate): void`\n- `describe(): Record<string, unknown>`: Serializable description (debug UI, `state()`).\n- `state(): CameraRigState`: The rig as data, exact (`engine.snapshot()`).\n- `setState(s: CameraRigState): void`: Put the rig back where `state()` found it (the same preset).\n\n### interface CameraRigState\n\nWhere a rig is and looks, as data (`engine.snapshot()`): the frame after a restore sees the camera the frame after the snapshot did.\n\n- `readonly preset: CameraPreset`\n- `readonly focus: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly zoom: number`\n- `readonly aspect: number`\n- `readonly position: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly quaternion: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly shaken: readonly number[]`\n- `readonly own: Readonly<Record<string, number>>`: What a preset keeps of its own (an orbit's yaw, pitch and distance).\n\n### createCameraRig()\n\n```ts\nfunction createCameraRig(config: CameraConfig = {}): 