- **Clients to host:** 15 Hz unreliable `{t:'i', n, x, z, tool, use, gift, steady}` (always sent, even idle; the harness requires traffic both ways).\",\"- **Clients to host:** 15 Hz `AIHub.send({t:'i', n, x, z, tool, use, gift, steady}, { to: AIHub.host, reliable: false })`, always sent, even idle (the harness requires traffic both ways).\")\n# paint cost, measured at full size\nrep(\"- ✔ Rewriting the whole buffer costs well under 1 ms a frame in JS.\",\"- ✔ Copying the base into the buffer and painting 60 blood discs measured about 0.5 ms a frame.\")\n# gear verified\nrep(\"- Show a DOM overlay with the exact text `Controller or keyboard required`, plus the engine's ⚙ (`settings: { button: 'top-right' }`, which opens the DOM panel).\",\"- Show a DOM overlay with the exact text `Controller or keyboard required`, plus the engine's ⚙: ✔ `settings: { button: 'top-right' }` shows it on a phone profile and a tap opens the DOM panel.\")\n# tendrils verified with the picture\nrep(\"✔ `SpringChain` driven per frame works for tendrils. **Place everything at `heightAt(x, z) + offset`** or the surface swallows it (✔ my mock's boss vanished inside the heart).\",\n    \"✔ My Dies Irae mock (a glowing eye in a crown of seven red plates, `rim` lit, with `SpringChain` tendrils trailing onto the heart) read very well. **Place everything at `heightAt(x, z) + offset`** or the surface swallows it: ✔ my first version sat at a fixed height and vanished inside the dome, and it needed `y` of about 3 over the dome's top.\")\n# vendor folder\nrep(\"tools/build.mjs\\ntests/        run.mjs\",\"vendor/       session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (from the hub, unmodified)\\ntools/build.mjs\\ntests/        run.mjs\")\n# cursor visibility\nrep(\"**Prompts follow the last input used,** switching the moment another device acts.\",\"**Pointer.** Hide the OS cursor over the canvas during surgery (you draw the reticle). In menus show it only while the mouse was the last device.\\n\\n**Prompts follow the last input used,** switching the moment another device acts.\")\n# charset lint\nrep(\"- Cast and sets referenced exist; every tool used; all text fits two wrapped lines at 342 art pixels wide; glyph tokens valid.\",\"- Cast and sets referenced exist; every tool used; all text fits two wrapped lines at 342 art pixels wide; every character is in the font's set; glyph tokens valid.\")\n# research line\nrep(\"- For the exit-interview file your brief asks for, `aiHub.genre` is `Simulation`\",\"- When you report research, say this plan's planner read the source's Wikipedia page, the hub's protocol and the engine kit, and ran engine spikes; add anything you did yourself.\\n- For the exit-interview file your brief asks for, `aiHub.genre` is `Simulation`\")\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nEOF\nsed -n 1,60p PLAN.md | cut -c1-200","description":"Apply review corrections to the plan and reread its opening"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0178av1xB6T9JNkL6iNisUJv","type":"tool_result","content":"# PLAN: a surgical drama in pixel art\n\nThe source is *Trauma Center: Second Opinion* (Atlus, Wii, North America 2006-11-19; a remake of *Trauma Center: Under the Knife*, DS, 2005). This plan is the full design and build guide for an origin\n\nYour brief wins if this plan conflicts with it. Pick the title the way the brief says; this plan never names one, and no story text may depend on it. All paths are relative to the dev folder.\n\n**How to read this plan**\n\n- **✔ means verified.** I ran it in this machine's Chromium (software WebGPU) on engine 0.46.0, from `file://` unless noted. Trust those facts and numbers. If the kit you download is newer, read its `\n- Everything else is a design decision. Refine the details if something plays better, but keep the goal each one serves.\n- Read the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md` whole, and search `API.md` for exact signatures.\n- Words used throughout:\n  - **field**: the operating surface.\n  - **art pixel**: one pixel of the engine's low-resolution picture.\n  - **seat**: one surgeon at the table, human or bot.\n  - **strain**: a boss pathogen.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** You are a clumsy second-year surgical resident in a rain-soaked port city. Your hands, a terrifying nurse in your ear and a talent you can't explain stand between your patients and a cure t\n\n- **Genre:** surgical action with visual-novel medical drama. `Simulation` is the nearest catalogue genre.\n- **Perspective:** first-person, surgeon's-eye, fixed close-up on a 3D operating field during surgery. Story scenes use talking-head close-ups and cinematic 3/4 shots of the rooms.\n- **Players:** 1 in the campaign. 1 to 4 in Consult, on one TV (couch) or on separate devices (AI Hub shared world).\n- **Length:** a first run takes about 80 minutes: 10 story operations plus scenes. One Consult round takes about 4 minutes.\n\n**Pillars**\n\n1. **Your hands are the game.** Each tool is a distinct physical verb with its own feel, sound and rumble.\n2. **Every second bleeds.** Vitals and the clock force constant small triage decisions.\n3. **Melodrama, played straight.** Hot-blooded rookie, icy rival, gentle villain, absurd stakes, nobody winks.\n4. **A second opinion.** Two doctors with two different gifts, and a second pair of hands at the table in co-op.\n\n**What the source's critics complained about, which we fix**\n\n- Unreliable gesture activation: gifts are a simple hold or press.\n- Difficulty spikes: three difficulties, per-stage retry, tunable balance.\n- Emotional distance from patients: every patient gets a chart hook and a follow-up line.\n- Minimal portraits: expressive 3D busts.\n\n**Originality is a hard rule.** Take the genre and the feel: precision surgery under a clock, a nurse barking orders, a man-made disease as a boss, a time-bending gift, melodrama. Do not use the sourc\n\nForbidden names include Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT, Kyriaki and the other day-named strains, and Healing Touch. The title's ri\n\n**The first five minutes**\n\n1. The title screen shows a dark operating theatre with one swinging lamp. An EKG line draws itself through the title.\n2. \"New Shift.\" Storm outside, 3 a.m. Nurse Reyes wakes Ilya with a clipboard to the head: a trawler deckhand has eleven fishhooks in one arm.\n3. The camera dives from a wide shot of the theatre onto the arm. Your gloved hand and an instrument appear. Reyes tells you what to do, in gibberish voice and text, with the correct button glyph on s\n4. You suck up blood with a satisfying slurp. You back the barbed hooks out along their curve, and each one tinks into the steel dish. The stitches rise in pitch as you zigzag.\n5. A rank stamp slams onto the chart: \"C. He'll keep the arm. Do better.\" A follow-up line says he made his daughter's wedding and danced badly.\n\nEvery system should serve that feeling.\n\n---\n\n## 2. World, cast and story","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8b716b92-81c2-4c62-8381-9131b2f861f1","uuid":"757c83e3-299b-4a10-a673-2b06139c65a3","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:15:28.849Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# PLAN: a surgical drama in pixel art\n\nThe source is *Trauma Center: Second Opinion* (Atlus, Wii, North America 2006-11-19; a remake of *Trauma Center: Under the Knife*, DS, 2005). This plan is the full design and build guide for an origin\n\nYour brief wins if this plan conflicts with it. Pick the title the way the brief says; this plan never names one, and no story text may depend on it. All paths are relative to the dev folder.\n\n**How to read this plan**\n\n- **✔ means verified.** I ran it in this machine's Chromium (software WebGPU) on engine 0.46.0, from `file://` unless noted. Trust those facts and numbers. If the kit you download is newer, read its `\n- Everything else is a design decision. Refine the details if something plays better, but keep the goal each one serves.\n- Read the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md` whole, and search `API.md` for exact signatures.\n- Words used throughout:\n  - **field**: the operating surface.\n  - **art pixel**: one pixel of the engine's low-resolution picture.\n  - **seat**: one surgeon at the table, human or bot.\n  - **strain**: a boss pathogen.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** You are a clumsy second-year surgical resident in a rain-soaked port city. Your hands, a terrifying nurse in your ear and a talent you can't explain stand between your patients and a cure t\n\n- **Genre:** surgical action with visual-novel medical drama. `Simulation` is the nearest catalogue genre.\n- **Perspective:** first-person, surgeon's-eye, fixed close-up on a 3D operating field during surgery. Story scenes use talking-head close-ups and cinematic 3/4 shots of the rooms.\n- **Players:** 1 in the campaign. 1 to 4 in Consult, on one TV (couch) or on separate devices (AI Hub shared world).\n- **Length:** a first run takes about 80 minutes: 10 story operations plus scenes. One Consult round takes about 4 minutes.\n\n**Pillars**\n\n1. **Your hands are the game.** Each tool is a distinct physical verb with its own feel, sound and rumble.\n2. **Every second bleeds.** Vitals and the clock force constant small triage decisions.\n3. **Melodrama, played straight.** Hot-blooded rookie, icy rival, gentle villain, absurd stakes, nobody winks.\n4. **A second opinion.** Two doctors with two different gifts, and a second pair of hands at the table in co-op.\n\n**What the source's critics complained about, which we fix**\n\n- Unreliable gesture activation: gifts are a simple hold or press.\n- Difficulty spikes: three difficulties, per-stage retry, tunable balance.\n- Emotional distance from patients: every patient gets a chart hook and a follow-up line.\n- Minimal portraits: expressive 3D busts.\n\n**Originality is a hard rule.** Take the genre and the feel: precision surgery under a clock, a nurse barking orders, a man-made disease as a boss, a time-bending gift, melodrama. Do not use the sourc\n\nForbidden names include Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Delphi, GUILT, Kyriaki and the other day-named strains, and Healing Touch. The title's ri\n\n**The first five minutes**\n\n1. The title screen shows a dark operating theatre with one swinging lamp. An EKG line draws itself through the title.\n2. \"New Shift.\" Storm outside, 3 a.m. Nurse Reyes wakes Ilya with a clipboard to the head: a trawler deckhand has eleven fishhooks in one arm.\n3. The camera dives from a wide shot of the theatre onto the arm. Your gloved hand and an instrument appear. Reyes tells you what to do, in gibberish voice and text, with the correct button glyph on s\n4. You suck up blood with a satisfying slurp. You back the barbed hooks out along their curve, and each one tinks into the steel dish. The stitches rise in pitch as you zigzag.\n5. A rank stamp slams onto the chart: \"C. He'll keep the arm. Do better.\" A follow-up line says he made his daughter's wedding and danced badly.\n\nEvery system should serve that feeling.\n\n---\n\n## 2. World, cast and story","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0178av1xB6T9JNkL6iNisUJv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
